{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a key player in the political landscape of the Levant during the 12th and 13th centuries. Raymond inherited the county from his father Raymond II in 1154 facing immediate challenges including ongoing conflicts with the Seljuk Turks and rival Crusader states. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and efforts to consolidate his power and territory.\n\n*Early Life and Accession to the County*\n\nRaymond was born around 1120 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Alice of Champagne. He received a solid education and was involved in political affairs from a young age. Upon his father's death Raymond ascended to the county inheriting a territory beset by external threats and internal instability. He faced immediate challenges from the Zengids a powerful Muslim dynasty that controlled much of Syria and Palestine. Raymond's early years were dedicated to defending Tripoli's borders and maintaining its independence. He also had to navigate complex relationships with other Crusader states including the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch.\n\nRaymond III engaged in numerous military campaigns against the Zengids and other enemies. He allied himself with other Crusader rulers forming a coalition to challenge Zengid power. He also invested in strengthening Tripoli's defenses constructing fortifications and building up its military forces. His reign saw periods of both conflict and relative peace. Raymond's diplomatic skills were crucial in maintaining stability and securing alliances. He often engaged in negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking to maintain trade relations and avoid costly wars.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a skilled diplomat who understood t